<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <docinfo>
    <brandlogo source="MLCLOGO.png" url="https://mlc.tamu.edu/"/>
    <latex-image-preamble>\usepackage{tikz}</latex-image-preamble>
  </docinfo>

  <article xml:id="session9wks">
    <title>MATH 152 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <worksheet>
      <title>Session #9: Power Series -- Sections 11.8 &amp; 11.9</title>

      <!-- PAGE 1: Warm-up problem 1 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Warm-Up</title>
          <p>Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts with peers around you.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise>
          <introduction>
            <p>Given that
              <me>\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(x-3)^n}{n}</me>
              has an interval of convergence of <m>[2,4)</m>, fill in the blanks for each of the following:</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement><p>The radius of convergence for <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(x-3)^n}{n}</m> is <m>R =</m> <fillin characters="9"/>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement><p>When <m>x = 3.5</m> <nbsp/> the series becomes <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(\qquad)^n}{n}</m></p><p>and the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/><nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement><p>When <m>x = 2</m> <nbsp/> the series becomes <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(\qquad)^n}{n}</m></p><p>and the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/><nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement><p>When <m>x = 4</m> <nbsp/> the series becomes <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(\qquad)^n}{n}</m></p><p>and the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/><nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement><p>When <m>x = 0</m> <nbsp/> the series becomes <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{(\qquad)^n}{n}</m></p><p>and the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/><nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 2: Warm-up problem 2 (number line) -->
      <page>
        <exercise>
          <introduction>
            <p>Suppose that <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n x^n</m> converges when <m>x = -3</m> and diverges when <m>x = 5</m>.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>Complete the figure below by writing <em>converges</em>, <em>diverges</em>, or <em>no conclusion</em> for each of the identified intervals:</p>
              <sidebyside width="80%" margins="10%">
                <image source="s9-numberline.png">
                  <description>Number line with tick marks at negative 5, negative 3, 0, 3, and 5. Dashed braces mark intervals above and below the line: above, the interval from negative 3 to 0 is labeled converges, closed bracket negative 3 comma 0; below, the interval from 5 to infinity is labeled diverges, open paren 5 comma infinity. Four more braces, over the intervals left of negative 5, from negative 5 to negative 3, from 0 to 3, and from 3 to 5, are unlabeled for students to fill in.</description>
                </image>
              </sidebyside>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.35in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n</m> is (<em>circle one</em>) inside <nbsp/> outside <nbsp/> the interval of convergence, so the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.35in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n (-2)^n</m> is (<em>circle one</em>) inside <nbsp/> outside <nbsp/> the interval of convergence, so the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.35in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n 7^n</m> is (<em>circle one</em>) inside <nbsp/> outside <nbsp/> the interval of convergence, so the series (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.3in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n (-4)^n</m> (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges <nbsp/> cannot be determined</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.3in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n (-9)^n</m> (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges <nbsp/> cannot be determined</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="0.3in">
            <statement><p>The series <m>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n 3^n</m> (<em>circle one</em>) converges <nbsp/> diverges <nbsp/> cannot be determined</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 3: Hands-on problem 1 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Hands-on</title>
          <p>Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts with peers around you.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise number="1" workspace="7in">
          <statement>
            <p>For what values of <m>x</m> does the power series
              <me>\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{n(x-2)^n}{n^2 + 1}</me>
              converge? Make sure to consider any endpoints.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 4: Hands-on problem 2 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="2" workspace="7in">
          <statement>
            <p>Find the interval and radius of convergence for</p>
            <me>\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{x^{2n}}{n(\ln(n))^2}</me>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 5: Hands-on problem 3 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="3">
          <introduction>
            <p>Find the interval and radius of convergence for</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3.5in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} (5x - 4)^n \cdot n!</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3.5in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{(5x - 4)^n}{n!}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 6: Hands-on problem 4 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="4">
          <introduction>
            <p><alert>Note:</alert> The geometric series formula is <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} ar^n = \frac{a}{1-r}</m> and this series converges for <m>|r| \lt 1</m>.</p>
            <p>We can use this to help find power series representations for certain functions, for instance <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{1-x} = \displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x^n</m></p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3.2in">
            <statement>
              <p>Use the above information to find a power series representation for
                <me>g(x) = \frac{1}{2x + 3}</me>
                and determine its radius of convergence.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3.2in">
            <statement>
              <p>Now find a power series representation for
                <me>h(x) = \frac{2}{4 + x^2}</me>
                and determine its radius of convergence.</p>
            </statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 7: Hands-on problem 5 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="5" workspace="7in">
          <statement>
            <p>Find the power series representation for
              <me>f(x) = \frac{x}{2x^2 + 3}</me>
            </p>
            <p><em>Hint</em>: <m>\dfrac{x}{2x^2 + 3} = \dfrac{x}{3} \cdot \left(\dfrac{1}{1 + \frac{2}{3}x^2}\right)</m></p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 8: Hands-on problem 6 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="6">
          <introduction>
            <p><alert>Note:</alert></p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li><p>The derivative (or integral) of a power series is another power series</p></li>
                <li><p>The radius of convergence remains the same when differentiating or integrating</p></li>
                <li><p>But the interval of convergence might change</p></li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
              <p>Use the geometric series formula to find a power series representation for
                <me>f(x) = -\frac{1}{(1-x)^2}</me>
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3in">
            <statement><p>What is the interval of convergence?</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 9: Hands-on problem 7 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="7" workspace="7in">
          <statement>
            <p>Recall that we found a power series representation for <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{2x+3}</m> in Question 4(a). Use this together with the fact that
              <me>\frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{1}{2x+3}\right] = \frac{-2}{(2x+3)^2}</me>
              to find a power series representation for
              <me>g(x) = \frac{x}{(2x+3)^2}</me>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 10: Hands-on problem 8 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="8" workspace="7in">
          <statement>
            <p>Use the power series representation for <m>\dfrac{1}{2x+3}</m> you found in Question 4(a) to find the power series representation for
              <me>f(x) = \ln(2x + 3)</me>
            </p>
            <p><em>Hint:</em> Notice that <m>f(0) = \ln(3)</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 11: Grades Up! problems 1-2 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Grades Up!</title>
          <p>Choose the best answer for each of the following.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise number="1">
          <statement>
            <p>Suppose the series <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} c_n (x-3)^n</m> converges when <m>x = 1</m> and diverges when <m>x = 7</m>.</p>
            <p>Given this information, which of the following statements can we conclude to be true?</p>
            <p>
              <ol marker="I.">
                <li><p>The radius of convergence is at least <m>2</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>The series converges when <m>x = 4.75</m></p></li>
                <li><p>The series diverges when <m>x = 6</m>.</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
            <p>
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>I and III only</p></li>
                <li><p>I and II only</p></li>
                <li><p>II and III only</p></li>
                <li><p>I, II, and III are all true.</p></li>
                <li><p>None of the statements are true.</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise number="2">
          <statement>
            <p>Given the geometric series <m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x^n = \frac{1}{1-x}</m> for <m>|x| \lt 1</m>, which series represents the derivative of <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{1-x}</m>?
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} n x^{n-1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n x^{n-1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty - 1} n x^{n-1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (1 - x)^{-2}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>None of these</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 12: Grades Up! problems 3-4 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="3">
          <statement>
            <p>The coefficient of <m>x^8</m> in the power series expansion of <m>\dfrac{2}{1 + 2x^2}</m> is:
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p><m>16</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>8</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>-16</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>-32</m></p></li>
                <li><p>None of these.</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise number="4">
          <statement>
            <p>If <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{1-x} = \displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x^n</m>, which of the following series represents <m>\displaystyle\int f(x)\,dx</m> ?
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{n+1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} n x^{n+1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>None of these</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

    </worksheet>
  </article>

</pretext>
